    <p class="lead">
      You have a JPG file and need to convert it. Should you choose PNG or WebP? The answer depends on what you're using the image for. Here's a complete comparison to help you decide.
    </p>

        <h2>Understanding the Two Formats</h2>
        <p>Before choosing, it helps to understand what makes PNG and WebP different under the hood.</p>

        <h3>PNG (Portable Network Graphics)</h3>
        <p>PNG has been the go-to format for high-quality images on the web since 1996. It uses lossless compression, which means every pixel in your original image is preserved exactly. PNG also supports alpha channel transparency, allowing you to have fully transparent or semi-transparent backgrounds. This makes it ideal for logos, icons, and any graphic that needs to sit cleanly on top of different backgrounds. The trade-off is file size — PNG files are typically larger than their JPG or WebP counterparts because no data is thrown away during compression.</p>

        <h3>WebP (Web Pictures)</h3>
        <p>WebP was developed by Google in 2010 and has become the modern standard for web images. It supports both lossy and lossless compression, giving you flexibility. Lossy WebP achieves 25-34% smaller file sizes than JPG at comparable quality. Lossless WebP matches PNG quality at 26% smaller file sizes. WebP also supports animation and alpha transparency, making it a superset of PNG's capabilities in many ways.</p>

