              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>

        <p>The numbers tell a clear story. Lossy WebP gives you the smallest files by far, while lossless WebP still beats PNG by about 26%. For web performance, that difference adds up fast — especially on mobile networks. For a deeper dive into compression numbers across multiple formats and quality settings, see our full <a href="/blog/image-compression-benchmarks" class="text-brand-600 dark:text-brand-400 hover:underline">image compression benchmarks</a>.</p>

        <h2>When to Choose PNG</h2>
        <p>Despite WebP's advantages, PNG still has important use cases:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Transparent logos and icons</strong> — When you need pixel-perfect transparency with no artifacts around edges</li>
          <li><strong>Screenshots and UI mockups</strong> — Sharp text and UI elements need lossless compression to stay crisp</li>
          <li><strong>Image editing workflows</strong> — When you're working in Photoshop or GIMP and need to preserve every detail across multiple saves</li>
          <li><strong>Print materials</strong> — When the image will be printed and any compression artifacts would be visible</li>
          <li><strong>Medical or scientific imaging</strong> — When data integrity is critical and no detail can be lost</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>When to Choose WebP</h2>
        <p>For most web use cases, WebP is the better choice:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Website hero images</strong> — Dramatically smaller files mean faster page loads and better Core Web Vitals scores</li>
          <li><strong>Product photos</strong> — E-commerce sites benefit from fast-loading product images across large catalogs</li>
          <li><strong>Blog post images</strong> — Every image on a blog post loads individually; smaller files improve reading experience</li>
          <li><strong>Social media content</strong> — Platforms compress images anyway; uploading WebP gives you a head start on quality</li>
          <li><strong>Thumbnails and previews</strong> — Lossy WebP at lower quality settings produces tiny files that still look great</li>
        </ul>

