          <li><strong><a href="/jpg-to-webp">JPG to WebP</a></strong> — Use our free converter when you want smaller files for web use without sacrificing visual quality</li>
          <li><strong><a href="/webp-to-png">WebP to PNG</a></strong> — Convert back to PNG when you need the file for software that doesn't support WebP</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>The Verdict: PNG vs WebP in 2026</h2>
        <p>For most people reading this, <strong>WebP is the better default choice</strong>. It produces smaller files, supports the same transparency features as PNG, and works in virtually every modern browser. The only time you should reach for PNG is when you specifically need lossless editing workflows, pixel-perfect transparency for logos, or compatibility with older software.</p>
        <p>The good news is you don't have to choose exclusively. Many websites serve WebP as the primary format with PNG as a fallback. Our <a href="/jpg-to-webp">JPG to WebP converter</a> and <a href="/jpg-to-png">JPG to PNG converter</a> make it easy to have both formats ready.</p>

